  "id": "Compound::DB00433 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Prochlorperazine is linked to Chrm2 through a series of molecular and pharmacological interactions that are mediated by the Phenothiazines class of compounds. The evidence suggests that Prochlorperazine, as part of the Phenothiazines family, binds to Chrm2, with Mesoridazine and Loxapine both exhibiting this interaction. These compounds are known to affect the nervous system, and their binding to Chrm2 is a common mechanism across different phenothiazines. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Prochlorperazine in modulating cholinergic receptor activity through its interaction with Chrm2. The evidence also indicates that Prochlorperazine may contribute to the pathophysiology of certain conditions, such as gait shuffling and sudden death, which are associated with Chrm2 dysfunction. The shared ancestry of Prochlorperazine and other phenothiazines, along with their common binding to Chrm2, supports a unified mechanism of action that is both pharmacologically and biologically relevant.",
